<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Utagawa Hiroshige</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a>–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Andō Hiroshige</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Utagawa Hiroshige was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e">ukiyo-e</a> artist, the last great master of the woodblock print in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. He is best known for landscape series such as The Fifty-three Stations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> (1833–1834) and One Hundred Famous Views of Edo (1856–1858), whose bold compositions and colour influenced European painters, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Van Gogh</a> and the Impressionists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/utagawa-hiroshige.jpg" alt="Utagawa Hiroshige" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: thesandiegomuseumofartcollection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memorial_Portrait_of_Utagawa_Hiroshige_(5765350019).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hiroshige was born Andō Tokutarō in the Yayosu Quay section of the Yaesu area of Edo, into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> family. His father, Gen'emon, was fire warden for the Yayosu Quay area. His mother died in early 1809 and his father later that year, after handing his fire warden duties to his twelve-year-old son, a post that left him much leisure. He studied Kanō-style painting under Okajima Rinsai, and, after Toyokuni had no room for him, joined the Utagawa school under Toyohiro. By 1812 he was permitted to sign his works under the art name Hiroshige. His early work included book illustrations and prints of beauties and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabuki">kabuki</a> actors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Not until 1829 to 1830 did Hiroshige begin the landscapes he is known for, such as the Eight Views of Ōmi. In 1832 he joined an official procession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, which let him sketch the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> route, and on his return he produced The Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō, which made his success. The designs drew on his travels along the whole 490 kilometres and included details of date, location and anecdotes of his fellow travellers. The series was reissued in three versions, one made jointly with Kunisada. He also produced forty-six of the seventy prints of The Sixty-nine Stations of the Kisokaidō, jointly with Keisai Eisen.</p><p>His subjects were atypical of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e">ukiyo-e</a>, whose usual focus was beautiful women, popular actors and the pleasure districts. Hokusai's Thirty-six Views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-fuji">Mount Fuji</a> influenced his choice of subject, though his approach was more poetic and ambient. He pioneered the vertical format in landscape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, and in his Edo series he often places large objects, people or animals in the foreground, a device derived from Western art. Hiroshige produced over 8,000 works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1856 Hiroshige retired from the world and became a Buddhist monk, the year he began his Edo series, which a wealthy Buddhist priest paid for in advance. He never lived in financial comfort. He died in the great Edo cholera epidemic of 1858, at the age of 62, and was buried in a Zen temple in Asakusa. For scholars and collectors his death marked the start of a rapid decline in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e">ukiyo-e</a>, especially under the westernisation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868. His work influenced European painting in the trend of Japonism: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Monet</a> and Manet collected and studied his compositions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Van Gogh</a> copied two of the Edo views.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hiroshige” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1836" aria-label="Edo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8080 875 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Utagawa Hiroshige"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8530 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8530 1145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1836">What was here in 1836? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
